  "worked_solution": "At $x=-2,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}g(x) & =g(-2) \\\\  & =-(-2) \\\\  & =2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}(a{x}^{2}+b) \\\\  & =a(-2)^{2}+b \\\\  & =4a+b.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=-2,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}g(x) \\\\ 2 & =4a+b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOn the other hand, at $x=0,$ the left-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}(a{x}^{2}+b) \\\\  & =a(0)^{2}+b \\\\  & =0+b \\\\  & =b,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) & =g(0) \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn order for $g(x)$ to be continuous at $x=0,$ we require that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to {0}^{-}}g(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to {0}^{+}}g(x) \\\\ b & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's solve the following system of equations\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4a+b=2 \\\\ b=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can replace $b$ by $6$ in the first equation. Therefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a+b & =2 \\\\ 4a+6 & =2 \\\\ 4a & =-4 \\\\ a & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $g(x)$ is continuous at $x=-2$ and $x=0$ for $a=-1$ and $b=6.$",
